# DAA LAB-6 — Set 1: Dynamic Programming

This folder contains complete, compiled-and-verified solutions to the four
Dynamic Programming problems from the **"DAA LAB-6 : Dynamic Programming"**
question sheet (see `questions.md` / `question_sheet.jpeg` for the original).

## Folder Structure

```
Set1_DynamicProgramming/
├── README.md                         <- this file
├── questions.md                      <- transcribed questions + embedded image
├── question_sheet.jpeg               <- original question sheet (image)
├── all_outputs.md                    <- consolidated, real program run transcripts
│
├── Q1_Fibonacci/
│   ├── fibonacci.c                   <- source code
│   └── ALGORITHM.md                  <- problem statement, recurrence, pseudocode,
│                                         complexity analysis, sample I/O
│
├── Q2_Knapsack01/
│   ├── knapsack.c
│   └── ALGORITHM.md
│
├── Q3_LCS/
│   ├── lcs.c
│   └── ALGORITHM.md
│
└── Q4_MatrixChainMultiplication/
    ├── mcm.c
    └── ALGORITHM.md
```

Each question has its **own folder** containing:
1. The C source code (fully commented, self-contained, reads input from
   stdin, prints the result **and** a complexity analysis to stdout).
2. An `ALGORITHM.md` with the problem statement, why DP applies (optimal
   substructure / overlapping subproblems), the recurrence relation,
   pseudocode, a full complexity derivation, and a worked sample.

All outputs from actually compiling and running the programs are
consolidated in **`all_outputs.md`** at the top level, so you can see real
results without needing a compiler.

## Question Summary

| # | Question | Algorithm | Time | Space |
|---|----------|-----------|------|-------|
| 1 | Nth Fibonacci number | Bottom-up tabulation / space-optimized / memoization | O(n) | O(n) or O(1) |
| 2 | 0/1 Knapsack | 2D DP table over (items × capacity) | O(n·W) | O(n·W) |
| 3 | Longest Common Subsequence | 2D DP table over string prefixes | O(m·n) | O(m·n) |
| 4 | Matrix Chain Multiplication | Interval DP over chain length | O(N³) | O(N²) |

## How to Compile & Run

Each `.c` file is standalone. From inside any `Qx_.../` folder:
```bash
gcc -O2 -Wall -o solution <filename>.c -lm
./solution
```
(`-lm` is only strictly needed for Q1 if you extend it with floating-point
math; it's harmless to always include.) All four programs read their input
interactively from stdin — see `all_outputs.md` for exactly what to type in,
using the assignment's own sample input as the primary test case.

## Verification Notes

- **Q4 (Matrix Chain Multiplication)** was tested against the exact sample
  input given in the assignment (`N=4, arr[]={10,30,5,60}`) and produces
  **4500**, matching the expected output on the question sheet exactly, with
  the required `O(N³)` time complexity.
- **Q3 (LCS)** was tested against the classic textbook example
  `X=AGGTAB, Y=GXTXAYB` → LCS = `"GTAB"`, length 4 — a well-known correct
  result.
- **Q2 (Knapsack)** was tested against a standard 4-item / capacity-50 case
  and correctly identifies the optimal item subset and profit.
- **Q1 (Fibonacci)** cross-checks three independent DP formulations
  (tabulation, space-optimized, memoization) against each other on every
  run — they always agree.

## Complexity Analysis — Why DP Helps

All four problems share the two properties that make Dynamic Programming
applicable:
- **Optimal substructure** — an optimal solution to the full problem is
  built from optimal solutions to smaller subproblems of the same kind.
- **Overlapping subproblems** — a naive recursive solution would recompute
  the exact same subproblem many times; DP computes each one exactly once
  and reuses the answer (via a table, for bottom-up, or a memo cache, for
  top-down).

This is why, e.g., Fibonacci drops from exponential `O(2ⁿ)` (naive
recursion) to linear `O(n)` (DP), and Matrix Chain Multiplication drops
from the Catalan-number-many parenthesizations (exponential) down to
`O(N³)`.

